{
 "slug": "far-from-the-madding-crowd",
 "meta": {
  "blurb": "Bathsheba Everdene turns down the shepherd who loves her, then inherits a farm and runs it alone. One careless joke, a valentine sent for fun, brings a rich farmer to her door. A soldier with a sword is waiting in the woods.",
  "bible": "Gabriel Oak: a young sheep farmer, honest, patient, loyal; plays the flute; later Bathsheba's shepherd and bailiff. Bathsheba Everdene: a beautiful, proud, independent young woman who inherits her uncle's farm at Weatherbury. William Boldwood: a rich, serious, middle-aged farmer, about forty, a bachelor. Sergeant Frank Troy: a handsome, careless soldier. Fanny Robin: a young servant girl on Bathsheba's farm who loves Troy. Liddy Smallbury: Bathsheba's young maid and companion. Joseph Poorgrass: a shy, timid farm worker. Jan Coggan: a wise, good-humoured farm worker. Pennyways: the dishonest bailiff whom Bathsheba dismisses. Norcombe Hill: where Gabriel keeps sheep. Weatherbury: the village and Bathsheba's farm. Casterbridge: the market town; its hiring fair, corn market and workhouse. Bath: the city where Bathsheba and Troy marry in secret. Lulworth Cove: the bay on the Dorset coast where Troy swims. Dorset. California: where Gabriel thinks of going. Malthouse: the old building where workers drink and talk. Valentine. Christmas. Voice and tone: warm, wise and rural, with gentle humour; deaths are stated plainly, with no gore; Boldwood shoots Troy at his Christmas party and is sentenced to death, then reprieved; set in the English countryside of the 1800s.",
  "chapters": [
   "The Girl with the Mirror",
   "A Shepherd's Question",
   "Over the Edge",
   "Fire at Weatherbury",
   "The Malthouse and the Road",
   "Marry Me",
   "A Calm Man Disturbed",
   "Too Much Clover",
   "The Soldier in the Wood",
   "Hands Off",
   "Mrs Troy",
   "The Storm in the Rick-Yard",
   "Fanny's Last Road",
   "A Coffin at the Inn",
   "Flowers and Rain",
   "A Widow's Year",
   "Christmas at Boldwood's",
   "One Shot",
   "The Sentence",
   "The Answer at the Cottage"
  ]
 },
 "beats": [
  {
   "n": 1,
   "scene": "Gabriel Oak among his flock on Norcombe Hill at sunrise",
   "summary": "Gabriel Oak, a young farmer, keeps a small flock of his own on Norcombe Hill and dreams of a larger one."
  },
  {
   "n": 2,
   "scene": "Gabriel plays a flute by his hut while two dogs lie near",
   "summary": "In the evenings Gabriel plays his flute by his hut, with an old sheepdog and a young one beside him."
  },
  {
   "n": 3,
   "scene": "A wagon piled with furniture rolls along the road below the hill",
   "summary": "One cold morning a wagon loaded with furniture goes by, and a pretty girl sits on top of the load."
  },
  {
   "n": 4,
   "scene": "A girl on a wagon holds up a small mirror and smiles",
   "summary": "Believing that nobody sees her, the girl takes out a mirror and admires her own face, and Gabriel smiles."
  },
  {
   "n": 5,
   "scene": "Gabriel hands coins to the toll keeper while the girl waits",
   "summary": "At the toll gate the girl has no money for the fee, so Gabriel quietly pays it for her."
  },
  {
   "n": 6,
   "scene": "The wagon rolls away with the girl's chin held high",
   "summary": "She does not thank him, and Gabriel is both amused and hurt by her pride."
  },
  {
   "n": 7,
   "scene": "Gabriel talks with a neighbour on a country lane",
   "summary": "Gabriel learns that the girl is Bathsheba Everdene, who has come to live with her aunt on a nearby farm."
  },
  {
   "n": 8,
   "scene": "Gabriel carries a motherless lamb in his coat across wet fields",
   "summary": "Gabriel chooses a motherless lamb and carries it down to the aunt's farm as a gift."
  },
  {
   "n": 9,
   "scene": "Bathsheba and her aunt sit in a farmhouse kitchen with a lamb by the fire",
   "summary": "Bathsheba thanks him politely but laughs at his clumsy words, and her aunt asks him to come again."
  },
  {
   "n": 10,
   "scene": "Gabriel climbs the hill alone under a sky full of stars",
   "summary": "Gabriel walks home certain that he has found the woman he will marry."
  },
